          %p
            %strong History.
          %p
            In 1852
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Faraday"} Faraday
            observes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+lines"} flux lines
            emanating from magnetic poles &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Faraday1852"}> Faraday 1852
            \&amp;rbrack;.
          %p
            In 1931
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/P.+A.+M.+Dirac"} Dirac
            invokes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            to argue that, if there were unpaired such (mono-)poles, then the total flux emanating from them — and thus the magnetic charge carried by them — had to come in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integer
            multiples of a unit quantum &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Dirac31"}> Dirac 1931
            \&amp;rbrack;.
          %p
            In 1957
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexei+Abrikosov"} Abrikosov
            essentially notices that the same electromagnetic flux-&amp;charge-quantization mechanism makes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abrikosov+vortex"} vortex strings
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superconductors"} type II superconductors
            carry units of localized magnetic flux. &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Abrikosov57a"}> Abrikosov 1957
            \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            In 1985
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Orlando+Alvarez"} Alvarez
            understands such
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soliton"} solitonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+fields"} magnetic fields
            as 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+ordinary+cohomology"}> differential
            )
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#Alvarez85a"} Alvarez 1985
          %p
            In 1988
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Krzysztof+Gaw%C4%99dzki"} Gawędzki
            observes that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            flux felt by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            must similarly be quantized as a 3-cocycle in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Gawędzki88"} Gawędzki 1988
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#FreedWitten99"} Freed &amp; Witten 1999
          %p
            In the 1990s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theorists
            hypothesize that the combined flux of
            = succeed "&" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
            and hence the charge of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            is analogously quantized in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology"} generalized
            cohomology theory called “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"}> topological K-theory
            ” &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#MinasianMoore97"}> Minasian &amp; Moore 1997
            ;
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#Witten98"} Witten 1998
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted
            = succeed "&rbrack;," do
              %a{:href => "#BouwknegtMathai01"} Bouwknegt &amp; Mathai 2001
          %p
            and that the flux of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} C-field
            and hence the charge of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-branes"} M-branes
            is quantized in a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shifted+C-field+flux+quantization"}> shifted half-integral
            ” cohomology theory &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Witten96a"}> Witten 1996
            \&amp;rbrack; whose proper mathematical home motivates
            %a{:href => "#HopkinsSinger05"} Hopkins &amp; Singer 2005
            but remains somewhat mysterious, for a long time.
          %p
            In the 2020s
            %a{:href => "#FSS20H"} Fiorenza et al. 2020
            develop a systematic understanding &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#FSS23"}> FSS23-Char
            \&amp;rbrack; of flux quantization of any
            %a{:href => "higher+gauge+field#HigherGaugeTheoryOfMaxwellType"} higher gauge theory of Maxwell-type
            in generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
            theory, using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+theorem+of+dg-algebraic+rational+homotopy+theory"} tools from dg-algebraic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
            related to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity"} "FDA-method" in the supergravity literature
          %p
            This provides a transparent re-derivation of known flux quantization laws and it allows to finally discuss
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} C-field
            flux- and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-brane"} M-brane
            charge-quantization.
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#Outline Overview
          %p
            %strong
              (
              %a{:href => "#FluxDensitiesAndBraneSources"}> §2
              )
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
            (review includes
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Alfonsi24"} Alfonsi 2024, §2
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BFJKNRSW24"}
                BFJ
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mrow
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^+
                2024
            %a{:href => "higher+gauge+field#HigherGaugeTheoryOfMaxwellType"} of Maxwell-type
            (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HigherMaxwellEquations"}
            below) is a (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"}> quantum
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            analogous to
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT+on+curved+spacetimes"}> on curved spacetimes
            ), but with the analog of the electromagnetic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+density"} flux density
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
            (
            %a{:href => "electromagnetic+field#FieldStrengthAsClosed2Form"} which ordinarily is
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+2-form"} differential 2-form
            on 3+1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+manifold"} dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^4
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+forms"} differential forms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ≡
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec F \,\equiv\, \big\{F^{(i)}\big\}_{i \in I}
            of any degree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi deg
                    %mi i
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} deg_i \geq 1
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+manifold"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              \-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^D
            of any dimension
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = d+1 \geq 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell%27s+equations"} Maxwell's equations
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:EqHigherMaxwellEquations"} (6)
          %p
            Such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theories"} higher gauge theories
            famously appear as the
            = succeed "-sector" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+field"} gauge field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            and hence in
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} super
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
          %p
            In analogy to how for ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell+theory"} Maxwell theory
            one may think of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
            or stable bumps in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+density"} flux density
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
            as being sourced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charges"} charges
            carried by (hypothetical)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+monopoles"} Dirac monopoles
            or by (observed)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abrikosov+vortex+strings"} Abrikosov vortex strings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
            or stable bumps in these higher flux densities as sourced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+branes"} singular branes
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitonic+branes"} solitonic branes
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membrane"} mem-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            carrying suitable higher charges.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong
              (
              %a{:href => "#FluxQuantizationLaws"}> §3
              )
            But for such
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+brane"} singular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitonic+branes"} solitonic branes
            to be “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+particle"}> elementary
            ” objects of individually discernible nature, their charges, and hence the total fluxes which they source, should have discrete (“quantized”) values (as indeed observed for
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abrikosov+vortex+strings"} Abrikosov vortex strings
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} flux quantization
            is about.
          %p
            Traditionally one declares the full higher gauge field to be given by gauge potentials
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi A
                    %mo ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat A
            whose curvature is the flux density
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec F
            and which is globally subjected to a topological condition that implies the flux and charge quantization. While this is classical for electromagnetism (and
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
          %p
            More systematically, one may understand
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} flux quantization
            as the specification of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} generalized
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"}> non-abelian
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
            for which the charges are required to be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Dold+character"} characters
          %p
            From this streamlined point of view the higher
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potential"} gauge potential
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+theory"} differential
            cohomology theory.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong
              (
              %a{:href => "#Examples"}> §4
              )
            Examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} flux quantization
            beyond
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
            play a key role in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the seminal “Hypothesis K” postulating
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge+quantization+in+K-theory"} D-brane charge quantization in K-theory
            %li
              %p
                the more recent “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Hypothesis+H"}> Hypothesis H
                ” postulating
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-brane"} M-brane
                charge quantization in unstable
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+Cohomotopy"} twisted
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy"} Cohomotopy
          %p
            These hypotheses are not unrelated: Under “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+reduction"} Kaluza-Klein reduction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber"} fiber
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} C-field
            fluxes on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mn 10
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{10+1}
            are to give rise to
            %em most
            of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                \-
            = succeed "-fluxes" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mn 9
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{9+1}
            (as part of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+between+M-theory+and+type+IIA+string+theory"} duality between M-theory and type IIA string theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Hypothesis+H"} Hypothesis H
            may be understood as providing an
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theoretic
            %em lift
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge+quantization+in+K-theory"} Hypothesis K
            to the extent that it reduces to the latter under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theoretic
            %em correction
            to the extent that it does not quite reduce to Hypothesis K.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Perspective.
            This highlights that a
            %em choice
            of flux quantization is (depending on perspective of how that higher gauge theory is ultimately defined):
          %ul
            %li a hypothesis about
          %p or else
          %ul
            %li a specification of
          %p
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative completion
            of the given
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
          %p
            Traditionally, flux quantization laws have been postulated sporadically and in ad-hoc fashion, in order to patch up “anomalous” theories: Since the ancient past it has been common to define any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} physical theory
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stationary+action+principle"} stationary action principle
            embodied by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST+complex"} BRST complex
            is extracted, whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#HenneauxTeitelboim92"} Henneaux &amp; Teitelboim 1992
            = succeed "\u201D)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomalies"} anomalies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
            was postulated to cure an anomaly in the quantum theory of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            propagating in the background field of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+monopole"} magnetic monopole
            (a “0-brane”), while the enigmatic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shifted+C-field+flux+quantization"} shifted C-field flux quantization
            similarly serves to cure an anomaly in the quantum theory of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            propagating in the background field of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            (
            %a{:href => "#Witten96a"}> Witten 1996a, §2.2
            ).
          %p
            More systematically, the available choices of flux-quantization laws
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebro-topologically
            determined by the form of the higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+law"} Gauss law
            on any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
            = succeed "-theory," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-stack"} stack
            of flux-quantized gauge fields. This process makes no reference to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+densities"} Lagrangian densities
            and applies seamlessly to field theories that do not even have a natural Lagrangian description, such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theories"} self-dual higher gauge theories
          %p Typically there is an “evident” choice of flux quantization and this is the choice tacitly made in the literature, where considered at all. But it is important to notice that there are other admissible choices, embodying hypotheses about (or definitions of) non-evident nonperturbative completions of the given higher gauge theory.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong The logic of flux quantization.
            The following table shows in outline the logic of algebro-topological flux quantization as reviewed here; on the left in generality and on the right for our running examples:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                traditional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
                (experimentally well-supported);
            %li
              %p
                traditional
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge+quantization+in+topological+K-theory"} D-brane charge quantization
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
                (“Hypothesis K”);
            %li
              %p
                more recent
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-brane"} M-brane
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shifted+C-field+flux+quantization"} charge quantization
                in unstable
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+Cohomotopy"} twisted
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy"} Cohomotopy
                (“
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Hypothesis+H"}> Hypothesis H
                ”).
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-FluxQuantizationSurvey-240123.jpg", :width => "900px"}/
          %p
            As this table indicates, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebro-topological
            nature of flux/charge quantization is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+Lie+theory"} higher Lie theoretic
            (explained in
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#FluxQuantizationLaws"} §3
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebras"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              \-algebras
            associated with a given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
            %a{:href => "higher+gauge+field#HigherGaugeTheoryOfMaxwellType"} of Maxwell type
            (
            %a{:href => "#FluxDensitiesAndBraneSources"}> §2
            ):
          %p
            %strong
              (i) Bianchi-Gauss
              = succeed "-algebras." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            The higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identities"} Bianchi identities
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "higher+gauge+field#HigherGaugeTheoryOfMaxwellType"} duality-symmetric higher flux densities
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+law"} Gauss law
            (Prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SolutionSpaceViaGaussLaw"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+densities"} flux densities
            jointly constitute a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+L-infinity+algebra+valued+differential+form"}
              closed
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in a characteristic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              \-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔞
