                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^4
            %em electric flux density
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
          %p
            Imagining, as Dirac did, that Faraday’s rod magnet could be made
            %em infinitely
            long and thin, any one of its poles would look like an isolated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monopole"} mono-pole
            with flux concentrating towards it from all directions:
          #eq:MagneticMonopoleSchematics.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MagneticMonopole-240124.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
          %p
            At the point of the idealized monopole itself, the flux density
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            per unit volume would diverge – a “singularity” much in the sense of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+holes"} black holes
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#TheDistinctionBetweenSingularAndSolitonicBranes"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{3,1}
            itself, but its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            around the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{0,1}
            of the would-be monopole:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∖
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} homeo
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ∖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} homeo
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msubsup
                    %mi R
                    %mrow
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 0
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} hmtp
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}^{3,1}
                  \\setminus
                  \\mathbb{R}^{0,1}
                  \\;
                  \\underset{\mathrm{homeo}}{\simeq}
                  \\;
                  \\mathbb{R}^{0,1}
                  \\times
                  \\big(
                  \\mathbb{R}^3 \setminus \{0\}
                  \\big)
                  \\;
                  \\underset{\mathrm{homeo}}{\simeq}
                  \\;
                  \\mathbb{R}^{0,1}
                  \\times
                  R^1_{\gt 0}
                  \\times S^2
                  \\;
                  \\underset{\mathrm{hmtp}}{\simeq}
                  \\;
                  S^2
                  \\,.
          %p
            As such, magnetic monopoles are the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+brane"} singular
              0-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            of electromagnetism (cf.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#TheDistinctionBetweenSingularAndSolitonicBranes"} below
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} symmetry
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/broken+symmetry"} broken symmetry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/grand+unified+theories"} grand unified symmetry
          %p
            On the other hand, the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitonic+branes"}> solitonic branes
            ” of electromagnetism (cf. again
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#TheDistinctionBetweenSingularAndSolitonicBranes"} below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"}> strings
            ) approximated by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nambu-Goto+action"} Nambu-Goto action
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "vortex+string#NielsenOlesen73"}> Nielsen &amp; Olesen 1973
            ;
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "vortex+string#Polyakov08"} Polyakov 2008, p. 1
            = succeed "&rbrack;):" do
              %a{:href => "vortex+string#BeekmanZaanen11"} Beekman &amp; Zaanen 2011
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abrikosov+vortices"} Abrikosov vortices
            formed in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superconductors"} type II superconductors
            within a transverse magnetic field:
          #eq:AbrikosovVortexSchematics.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SuperconductorVortexStructure-230304.jpg", :width => "720px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                adapted from
                %a{:href => "superconductivity#LoudonMidgley09"} Loudon and Midgley 2009
          %p
            In this case the “sphere” through which the total magnetic flux density is measured is nominally the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            filled by the superconducting material, but since far away from any vortex the magentic flux has to vanish, this plane appears to the fluxes via its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-point+compactification"} one-point compactification
            with the “point at infinity” adjoined.
          %p
            These vortex strings are
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitons"} solitons
            in that the flux density is everywhere finite, and yet the “bumps” in the flux density are topologically stable. Much like a bump in a rug cannot be flattened as long as the boundary of the rug is fixed in place, so the requirement that flux densities “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vanishing+at+infinity"}> vanish at infinity
            ” keeps the vortex strings in place — or at least this is the case once we take account of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+flux+quantization"} Dirac flux quantization
            below.
          %p In summary:
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-MonopolesAndVortices-240124.jpg", :width => "750px"}/
          %p#HomotopicalEMFluxQuantization
            The spacetime domains on which to exhibit fluxes of singular EM-branes (monopoles) and of solitonic EM-branes (vortices) are as shown on the left of the following graphics, respectively, whose right hand side already shows the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+maps"} classifying maps
            of their quantized charges, further discussed
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#FluxQuantizationLaws"} below
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-ChargeOfMonopolesAndVortices.jpg", :width => "800px"}/
          %p The fact that there are, generally, these two kinds of “branes imprinted on flux” is “well-known” and yet underappreciated:
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#TheDistinctionBetweenSingularAndSolitonicBranes Singular versus solitonic branes
          %p
            Generally, imprinted on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+densities"} flux densities
            may be
            %em two kinds
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+branes"} singular branes
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+branes"}> black branes
                ) reflected in
                %strong diverging flux density
                at
                %strong singular loci
                that are to be removed from spacetime,
              %blockquote
                %p
                  Beware that in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
                  these are also called “elementary branes” &amp;lbrack;
                  %a{:href => "#DuffLu94"}> Duff &amp; Lu 1994
                  \&amp;rbrack;, in reference to how
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+holes"} black holes
                  carry the same quantum numbers as
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+particles"} elementary particles
                  – but here we rather not conflate these two aspects.
            %li
              %p
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitonic+branes"} solitonic branes
                reflected in
                %strong finite flux density
                which is localized in that it
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vanishing+at+infinity"} vanishes at infinity
              %blockquote
                %p
                  The terminology “
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitonic+brane"}> solitonic brane
                  ” was introduced in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "solitonic+brane#DuffKhuriLu92"} Duff, Khuri &amp; Lu 1992
                  %a{:href => "solitonic+brane#DuffKhuriLu94"} 1994
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "solitonic+brane#DuffLu93"} Duff &amp; Lu 1993
                  %a{:href => "#DuffLu94"} 1994
                  to mean stable but non-singular brane-like solutions to (supergravity/flux) equations of motion (“
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitons"}> solitons
                  ”).
          %p
            This general distinction between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+branes"} singular branes
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitonic+branes"} solitonic branes
            is important for the correct identification of the implications of choices of
            = succeed "-laws" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} flux quantization
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Spacetime domains for brane fluxes.
            More formally, one may encode these two cases by slightly adjusting the nature of the
            %strong spacetime domain
            on which fluxes are actually defined &amp;lbrack;cf.
            = succeed "&rbrack;:" do
              %a{:href => "#SS23-MF"} SS23-MF, §2.1
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                fluxes sourced by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+branes"} singular branes
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p+1
                inside
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{d+1}
                are actually defined on the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ∖
                      %msup
                        %mi Q
                        %mrow
                          %mi p
                          %mo +
                          %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{d+1} \setminus Q^{p+1}
                of their singular
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            %li
              %p
                fluxes sourced by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitonic+branes"} solitonic branes
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d-p
                are actually defined on their transverse space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi T
                        %mrow
                          %mi d
                          %mo −
                          %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^{d-p}
                equipped with a “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+at+infinity"}> point at infinity
                ” on which they are required to vanish.
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-DomainsForSingAndSolFluxes.240125.jpg", :width => "620px"}/
          %p
            The condition of flux densities
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vanishing+at+infinity"} vanishing at infinity
            on some space is naturally formalized by considering the larger
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, x \in X)
            (we discuss further below how to properly speak of differential geometric smoothness in this context) and regarding their given “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+point"}> base point
            ” as being the “point at infinity”, whence we shall write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X, \infty_X)
            for the generic pointed space. Then a function “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vanishing+at+infinity"}> vanishing at infinity
            ” on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\infty_X)
            is a function on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            that literally vanishes at
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mn ∞
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty_X
          %p For example:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The result of
                %em adjoining
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
                its “point at infinity” (this is called its
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-point+compactification"} one-point compactification
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mrow
                            %mo ∪
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn ∞
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n_{\cup \{\infty\}}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homeomorphism"} homeomorphic
                to the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-sphere"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                  \-sphere
                with any basepoint:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∪
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mi n
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi homeo
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathbb{R}^n_{\cup \{\infty\}}
                      \\,\underset{homeo}{\simeq}\,
                      S^n
                      \\,.
            %li
              %p
                On the other hand, to consider unconstrained functions on some
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                in this context, we may regard all the points of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                as being at finite distance by declaring that the “point at infinity” is disjoint from
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint union
                (denoted “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊔
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqcup
                ” as opposed to “
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∪
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cup
                ”):
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊔
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊔
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      X_{\sqcup \{\infty\}}
                      \\;\coloneqq\;
                      X \sqcup \{ \infty \}
                      \\,.
          %p
            Given two such pointed spaces, their
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
            “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∧
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \wedge
            ” is their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            with all points that are at infinity in either factor identified with a single new point at infinity:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mn ∞
                    %mi Y
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ×
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mn ∞
                        %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ∪
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mn ∞
                        %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\big(
                  X,\, \infty_X
                  \\big)
                  \\wedge
                  \\big(
                  Y,\, \infty_Y
                  \\big)
                  \\;\;
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\;\;
                  \\frac{
                  X \times Y
                  }{
                  X \times \{\infty_Y\}
                  \\,\cup\,
                  \\{\infty_X\} \times Y
                  }
          %p
            %br/
          %p
          #FlatBranes.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (The case of flat branes.)
              %br/
              In the case of
              %em flat branes
              — i.e. with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolumes"} worldvolumes
              inside
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
              — both these spacetime domains are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalent
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spheres"} spheres
            %p
              (1.) The spacetime domain for flat
              %em singular
              branes is homotopy-equivalent to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+sphere"} unit sphere
              in the transverse space, hence the sphere
              %em around
              the singular brane locus:
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 0px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-FlatSingularBranes-240124.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
            %p
              (2.) The spacetime domain for flat solitonic branes is homotopy equivalent to the sphere which is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-point+compactification"} one-point compactification
              of the transverse space (its
              = succeed "):" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stereographic+projection"} stereographic projection
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 0px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-FlatSolitonicBranes-240124.jpg", :width => "800px"}/
            %p
          %p
          #FlatBranesOfElectromagnetism.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (the flat branes of electromagnetism)
              %br/
              Specifying Ex.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FlatBranes"}
              to the case of ordinary electromagnetic flux (
              %a{:href => "#OrdinaryElectromagneticFluxAndItsBranes"}> above
              ) it follows from this general reasoning that a flux density 2-form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mn 3
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D=3+1
              may reflect the presence of
            %ol
              %li
                %p singular 0-branes with spacetime domain
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mrow
                            %mn 3
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ∖
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mrow
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %munder
                          %mo ≃
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} homeo
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mrow
                            %mn 0
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ×
                        %msub
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mrow
                            %mo &gt;
                            %mn 0
                        %mo ×
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 2
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %munder
                          %mo ≃
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} hmtp
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\mathbb{R}^{3,1} \setminus \mathbb{R}^{0,1}
                        \\,\underset{\mathrm{homeo}}{\simeq}\,
                        \\mathbb{R}^{0,1} \times \mathbb{R}_{\gt 0} \times S^2
                        \\,\underset{\mathrm{hmtp}}{\simeq}\,
                        S^2
              %li
                %p solitonic 1-branes with spacetime domain
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mo +
                          %mrow
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ∧
                        %msubsup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mrow
                            %mo ∪
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn ∞
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                          %mn 2
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %munder
                          %mo ≃
                          %mi homeo
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msubsup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mo +
                          %mrow
                            %mn 1
                            %mo ,
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ∧
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 2
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %munder
                          %mo ≃
                          %mi hmtp
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\mathbb{R}^{1,1}_+
                        \\wedge
                        \\mathbb{R}^{2}_{\cup \{\infty\}}
                        \\;\underset{homeo}{\simeq}\;
                        \\mathbb{R}^{1,1}_+
                        \\wedge
                        S^2
                        \\;\underset{hmtp}{\simeq}\;
                        S^2
            %p
              which are exactly the familiar cases of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+monopoles"} magnetic monopoles
              (hypothetical) and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abrikosov+vortex+strings"} Abrikosov vortex strings
              (observed), discussed
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#OrdinaryElectromagneticFluxAndItsBranes"} above
          %p
          #NearHorizonGeometriesOfSingularBranes.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong> (near-horizon geometries of singular branes
              )
              %br/
              The idea of regarding singular branes from the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
              of their singular locus in spacetime is familiar from the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+correspondence"} AdS/CFT correspondence
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/near+horizon+geometry"} near horizon geometry
              of any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gt 1/4
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BPS+state"} BPS
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+brane"} black brane
              are all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/product+topological+spaces"} product spaces
              of an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti de Sitter spacetime
              with a (
              %a{:href => "group+actions+on+spheres#FreeActionsByFiniteGroupsAndSphericalSpaceForms"} free discrete quotient
              of) a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
              (a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spherical+space+form"} spherical space form
              = succeed "&rbrack;." do
                %a{:href => "near-horizon+geometry#AFFHS98"} Acharya, Figueroa-O’Farrill, Hull &amp; Spence 1999
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FlatBranes"}
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-NearHorizonGeometries.jpg", :width => "600px"}/
            %p
          %p In general, the spacetime domain on which to measure flux densities may be a mix of these purely singular and purely solitonic situations, in which case the notions of singular and of solitonic branes blend into each other:
          %p
          #SolitonicBranesInKKCompactifications.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (solitonic branes in KK-compactifications)
            %p
              In mild generalization of Ex.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FlatBranes"}
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
              over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi D
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≡
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi d
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X^D
                    \\,\equiv\,
                    \\mathbb{R}^{1,d-1} \times S^1
                    \\,.
            %p
              In this case the flux sourced by solitonic branes of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              as seen on the base spacetime
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi d
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{1,d-1}
              is measured on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
              space
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                    %msub
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi homeo
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msubsup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊔
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∧
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi homeo
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msubsup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊔
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∧
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\big(
                    S^1 \times \mathbb{R}^n
                    \\big)_{\cup \{\infty\}}
                    \\;\underset{homeo}{\simeq}\;
                    S^1_{\sqcup \{\infty\}}
                    \\wedge
                    \\mathbb{R}^n_{\cup \{\infty\}}
                    \\;\underset{homeo}{\simeq}\;
                    S^1_{\sqcup \{\infty\}}
                    \\wedge
                    S^n
                    \\,.
            %p
              This was maybe first understood by
              %a{:href => "#BergmanGimonHořava99"} Bergman, Gimon &amp; Hořava 1999, §2.2 &amp; §2.3
              (there in slightly different language).
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#HigherFluxAndItsBranes Higher fluxes and their brane sources
          %p
            On this backdrop of ordinary electromagnetic flux (
            %a{:href => "#OrdinaryElectromagneticFluxAndItsBranes"}> above
            ) and of the general rule for measuring flux sourced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+branes"} singular branes
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitonic+branes"} solitonic branes
            (
            %a{:href => "#TheDistinctionBetweenSingularAndSolitonicBranes"}> above
            ) it clearly makes sense to consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} physical theories
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
            whose precise nature remains to be discussed, but whose flux densities are reflected in higher-degree differential forms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi F
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^{(i)}(X^D)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+set"} index set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi FinSet
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \in FinSet
            and jointly to be denoted as follows:
          #eq:TheHigherFluxDensities.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≡
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi dR
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi deg
                        %mi i
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\vec F
                  \\;\equiv\;
                  \\Big\{
                  F^{(i)}
                  \\,\in\,
                  \\Omega_{dR}^{deg_i}\big(
                  X^D
                  \\big)
                  \\Big\}
                  \\,.
          #eq:FluxesAndTheirSingularSources.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (4)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-FluxesAndTheirSingularBranes-240125.jpg", :width => "460px"}/
          %p
            Remarkably, such higher flux densities “automatically” appear in higher dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpartners"} superpartners
            = succeed "-field" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitino"} gravitino
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graviton"} graviton
            itself. In particular in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D10+supergravity"} D=10 supergravity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+supergravity"} D=11 supergravity
            these higher flux densities are known under the (now) fairly standard symbols shown on the right, along with the standard name of the correspoding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+branes"} singular branes
            (the “higher-dimensional monopoles”).
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D10+supergravity"}> D=10 supergravity
                \:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↔
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leftrightarrow
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NS5-brane"} NS5-brane
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F1-brane"} F1-brane
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↔
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leftrightarrow
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+supergravity"}> D=11 supergravity
                \:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-field"} C-field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ↔
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leftrightarrow
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
          %p
            (cf.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BlumenhagenLüstTheisen13"} Blumenhagen, Lüst &amp; Theisen 2013, §18.5, Table 18.3
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (singular vs. solitonic D-branes)
              %br/
              Historically, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D10+supergravity"} D=10 supergravity
              were first identified as such &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#Polchinski95"}> Polchinski 1995, cf. (14)
              \&amp;rbrack; in their
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+brane"} singular brane
              incarnation &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#DuffLu94"}> Duff &amp; Lu 1994
              \&amp;rbrack;, while their incarnation as solitonic branes (in the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#TheDistinctionBetweenSingularAndSolitonicBranes"} above sense
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge+quantization+in+K-theory"} D-brane charge quantization in K-theory
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#BergmanGimonHořava99"}> Bergman, Gimon &amp; Hořava 1999, §2.2 &amp; §2.3
              , building on earlier discussion by
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "#Witten98"} Witten 1998, §4.1
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#Examples"} below
            %p But beware that the distinction between these two aspects of D-branes, while “well-known”, is not commonly highlighted in the literature (not even in these original articles).
          %p The above flux densities in 11d and 10d are closely related:
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
                of fluxes form 11d to 10d)
              %br/
              Consider the case of C-field flux densities
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_4
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_7
              on an 11-dimensional spacetime
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mn 11
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{11}
              which is the total space of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+U%281%29-bundle"} principal U(1)-bundle
            %svg{:height => "51.395pt", :version => "1.2", :viewbox => "0 0 62.801 51.395", :width => "62.801pt", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
              %defs
                %g
                  %symbol{:id => "M7aNMOKvsy0-MfCxP9c3KhqAlGc=-glyph0-0", :overflow => "visible"}
