              embodies the density of an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+current"} electric current
              carrying an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+field"} electric field
              and inducing a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+field"} magnetic field
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
              source term, where the source is not given by (a polynomial in) the flux densities themselves, does not fit into the Definition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HigherMaxwellEquations"}
              and shall be disregarded for the purpose of the present discussion, meaning that we focus on the special case of Maxwell’s equations “in vacuum”:
            %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 0px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-MotionOfVacuumElectromagnetism.jpg", :width => "430px"}/
            %p
          %p
            It is clear that, mathematically at least, Ex.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MotionOfElectromagneticField"}
          %p
          #MotionOfUnboundedRRFields.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (Motion of unbounded
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
                fluxes)
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
              fluxes in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D10+supergravity"} D=10 supergravity
              in the case of vanishing
              = succeed "-fluxes" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
              = succeed "):" do
                %a{:href => "#MkrtchyanValach23"} Mkrtchyan &amp; Valach 2023
            #eq:PureUnboundedRREquations.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (8)
              %code.maruku-mathml
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 0px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-MotionOfPureRRFluxes.jpg", :width => "780px"}/
            %blockquote
              %p
                (While we may think of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo •
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
                as ranging over all
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
                – which is suggestive in view of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge+quantization+in+topological+K-theory"} Hypothesis K
                discussed below – of course on the given spacetime manifold of dimension
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %mn 10
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D=10
                all differential forms of degree
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 10
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gt 10
                vanish identically.)
            %p
              and, more generally, those with non-vanishing
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
              as follows:
            #eq:TwistedUnboundedRREquations.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (9)
              %code.maruku-mathml
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 0px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-UnboundedBRRFluxMotion.jpg", :width => "780px"}/
            %p
              Beware, while these equations are now often stated in this form, and while this is the form that motivates the traditional
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge+quantization+in+topological+K-theory"} Hypothesis K
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D10+supergravity"} D=10 supergravity
              (namely from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
              of
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+supergravity"} D=11 supergravity
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  The fluxes
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 10
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{10}
                  are not actually present (they arise only in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/massive+type+IIA+supergravity"} massive type IIA supergravity
              %li
                %p
                  The flux
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mn 7
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_7
                  has a non-linear
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identity"} Bianchi identity
                  (
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mn 7
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo =
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo −
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 4
                        %mo ∧
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 4
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ∧
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 6
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d} H_7 \,=\, -F_4 \wedge F_4 + F_2 \wedge F_6
                  ) which does not fit the suggestive pattern.
            %p
              For more on this see Ex.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MotionOfCoupledBRRFields"}
              below.
          %p
            Notice that in type IIB,
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:PureUnboundedRREquations"} (8)
            describes a flux density (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_5
            ) which is Hodge dual (not just to any other flux in the tuple but) to itself,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 5
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_5 \,=\, \star\, F_5
          %p
          #MotionOfSelfDualHigherGaugeFluxes.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (Motion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+field"} self-dual higher gauge field
                fluxes)
            %p
              Since Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HigherMaxwellEquations"}
              regards
              %em every
              higher gauge theory (of Maxwell-type) as being “self-dual” in a sense, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+densities"} flux densities
              of actual
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+fields"} self-dual higher gauge fields
              – in the strict sense that one and the same flux density form is required to be Hodge dual to itself – are readily an example of Def.
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HigherMaxwellEquations"}
            #eq:SelfDualHigherFluxEquationsOfMotion.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (10)
              %code.maruku-mathml
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 0px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-MotionOfSelfDualFlux.jpg", :width => "510px"}/
            %p
              Due to the properties of the square of the Hodge operator
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:HodgeSquareOnLorentzianSpacetime"} (7)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi deg
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} deg = 2k+1
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                      %mo =
                      %mn 4
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 4k + 2
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
          %p
            What is much more clear-cut than the example of fluxes in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D10+supergravity"} D=10 supergravity
            (Ex.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MotionOfUnboundedRRFields"}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+supergravity"} D=11 supergravity
          %p
          #MotionOfSupergravityCField.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (Motion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} C-field
                fluxes)
            %p
              The equations of motion of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} C-field
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+supergravity"} D=11 supergravity
              (originally the “3-index A-field” due to
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "D=11+supergravity#CremmerJuliaScherk78"} Cremmer, Julia &amp; Scherk 1978
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "D=11+supergravity#DAuriaFré82"} D’Auria &amp; Fré 1982, p. 131
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "D=11+supergravity#CastellaniDAuriaFré91"} Castellani, D’Auria &amp; Fré 1991, §III.8
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "D=11+supergravity#MiemiecSchnakenburg06"} Miemiec &amp; Schnakenburg 2006, p. 32
              = succeed "):" do
                %a{:href => "duality-symmetric+higher+gauge+theory#PremetricCFieldReferences"} there
            #eq:CFieldEquationsOfMotion.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (11)
              %code.maruku-mathml
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 0px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-MotionOfCField.jpg", :width => "580px"}/
            %p
          %p
          #MotionOfCoupledBRRFields.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (Motion of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+supergravity"} type IIA
                = succeed "&" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
                fluxes)
            %p
              Under
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
              of the C-field flux from Ex.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MotionOfSupergravityCField"}
              along a
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle"} bundle
              as in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:KKComponentsOfCFieldFlux"} (5)
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CFieldEquationsOfMotion"} (11)
              of the C-field from are equivalently expressed in terms of its
              = succeed "&" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B
              = succeed "-components" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "duality+between+M-theory+and+type+IIA+string+theory#MathaiSati04"} Mathai &amp; Sati 2004, §4
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "#FSS17SphereCocycles"} FSS17-Sph, §3
              = succeed "&rbrack;:" do
                %a{:href => "#Figueroa-O'FarrillSimón03"} Figueroa-O’Farrill &amp; Simón 2003, §1.2
            #eq:BRRFieldEquationsOfMotion.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (12)
              %code.maruku-mathml
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-DoubleDimReductionOfCField-240127.jpg", :width => "810px"}/
            %p
              These are the equations of motion of the flux densities of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+supergravity"} type IIA supergravity
              in their duality-symmetric formulation &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#CremmerJuliaLuPope98"}> Cremmer, Julia, Lu &amp; Pope 1998, §3
              \&amp;rbrack;.
            %p
              Several terms in
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:BRRFieldEquationsOfMotion"} (12)
              deserve special attention, either for how they appear or for how they do
              %em not
              appear:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  The Hodge dual
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 8
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ≔
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ⋆
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_8 \,\coloneqq\, \star \, F_2
                  is
                  %em not
                  part of the C-field in 11d, but is part of the gravitational field (the Hodge star encodes the 10d metric and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
                  is an aspect of the 11d fiber geometry). The expression for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %msub
                          %mi H
                          %mn 8
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d} H_8
                  arises as part of the gravitational field equations &amp;lbrack;
                  %a{:href => "#CremmerJuliaLuPope98"}> Cremmer, Julia, Lu &amp; Pope 1998 (3.4)
                  \&amp;rbrack;.
              %li
                %p
                  The presence in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+supergravity"} type IIA supergravity
                  of the non-linear Bianchi identity for
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mn 7
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_7
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#CremmerJuliaLuPope98"} CJLP98 (3.4)
                  %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:TwistedUnboundedRREquations"} (9)
                  from Ex.
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MotionOfUnboundedRRFields"}
                  – which it
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em breaks
              %li
                %p
                  No flux densities
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0
                  nor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mn 10
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{10}
                  appear in 10d from
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-reduction"} KK-reduction
                  on a circle, nor are they part of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+supergravity"} type IIA supergravity
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/massive+type+IIA+supergravity"} massive type IIA supergravity
                  whose relation to
                  = succeed "/" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+supergravity"} D=11 supergravity
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
                  remains less understood, see the references
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "massive+type+IIA+string+theory#ReferencesLiftToMTheory"} here
            %p
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#SolutionSpaceOfHigherMaxwellEquations Solution space of the flux equations
          %p
            %strong The phase space.
            Abstractly, the
            %em phase space
            of a any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            is nothing but the space of all those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+histories"} field histories
            that satisfy the given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"}> on-shell
            ” field histories). Phrased this way, this is sometimes called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliation
            of spacetime by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surfaces"} Cauchy surfaces
            has been or needs to be made.
          #eq:CanonicalAndCovariantPhaseSpace.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (13)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -10px 0px 10px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CanonicalAndCovariantPhaseSpace-240124.jpg", :width => "610px"}/
          %p
            The more traditionally familiar
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+phase+space"} canonical phase space
            (common physics jargon, somewhat incompatible with the mathematician’s “canonical”) is instead a parameterization of the covariant phase space by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+value+data"} initial value data
            on a choice of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetimes"} globally hyperbolic spacetimes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+value+data"} initial value data
            the essentially unique
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+history"} field history
            that evolves from it (possibly up to
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
          %p
            %strong Solution space of on-shell flux densities.
            At this point in the discussion, the full gauge field content is not yet determined – this will only be implied by a choice of flux quantization
            %a{:href => "#FluxQuantizationLaws"} below
            – so far we are only considering the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+densities"} flux densities
            of the would-be gauge fields. To remember this, we shall call the space of flux densities solving their equations of motion (Def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HigherMaxwellEquations"}
            = succeed ";" do
              %em solution space
            %em canonical solution space
            of initial value data on a Cauchy surface. But this goes a long way, since the higher Maxwell-type equations of motion constrain exclusively the flux densities: Once the flux-quantization the canonical phase will simply consist of all flux-quantized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potentials"} gauge potentials
            compatible with the flux densities in the canonical solution space.
          %p
          #SolutionSpaceViaGaussLaw.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a{:href => "#SS23-FQ"}> SS23-FQ
                )
              On a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetime"} globally hyperbolic spacetime
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi D
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ≃
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mrow
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi d
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^D \,\simeq\, \mathbb{R}^{0,1} \times X^d
              %a{:href => "higher+gauge+field#HigherGaugeTheoryOfMaxwellType"} higher Maxwell-equations
              of motion (Def.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HigherMaxwellEquations"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              to the solution of (just) the duality-symmetric
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identities"} Bianchi identities
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:EqHigherMaxwellEquations"} (6)
              restricted (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+of+differential+forms"} pulled back
              to) to any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ι
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi d
                      %mo ↪
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota \,:\, X^d \hookrightarrow X^D
              = succeed ":" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gau%C3%9F+law"} Gauß law
            #eq:GaussLawFromBianchiIdentities.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (14)
              %code.maruku-mathml
            %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 5px 0px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-HigherMaxwellSolutionSpace.jpg", :width => "850px"}/
            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (Solution- and phase-space of ordinary electromagnetism)
              %br/
              In the case of ordinary vacuum
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SolutionSpaceViaGaussLaw"}
              applied to the ordinary Maxwell equations from Ex.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MotionOfElectromagneticField"}
              says that the initial value data on a Cauchy surface
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^3
              is given by
              %em independently
              specifying magnetic and electric flux densities
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi E
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi dR
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    B,\, E \,\in\, \Omega^2_{dR}(X^3)
            %p
              subject only to the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a{:href => "Gauss+law#InElectromagnetism"} ordinary Gauß laws
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi E
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mn 0
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathrm{d} B \,=\, 0
                    \\,,\;\;\;
                    \\mathrm{d} E \,=\, 0
                    \\,.
            %p
              Indeed, the actual
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
              of electromagnetism, after introducing a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potential"} gauge potential
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "electromagnetism#ReferencesPhaseSpaceAndPoissonBrackets"} there
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+coordinate"} canonical coordinate
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potential"} gauge potential
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi A
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+momentum"} canonical momentum
                  the electric flux density
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi E
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %p
              Thereby
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≡
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi curv
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi A
                      %mo ^
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \,\equiv\, curv\big(\widehat{A}\big)
              is indeed independent from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              (and satisfies its Gauß law definitionally, while the Gauß law on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              is a phase space
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constrained+mechanics"} constraint
            %p
              Notice how, thereby, this traditional split of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+value+data"} initial value data
              into
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+coordinates"} canonical coordinates
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+momenta"} canonical momenta
              (whose definition requires assumption and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variation
              of a
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SolutionSpaceViaGaussLaw"}
              = succeed ")," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MotionOfElectromagneticField"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
              (since
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi ι
                        %mo *
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B = \iota^\ast F_2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_2
              (since
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi E
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi ι
                        %mo *
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E = \iota^\ast G_2
          %p
          #GravityDecouplesOnCanonicalPhaseSpace.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (Gravity “decouples” on canonical phase space)
              %br/
              The inverse isomorphism
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GaussLawFromBianchiIdentities"} (14)
              is given by time evolution of initial value data. Notice that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudo-Riemannian+metric"} pseudo-Riemannian metric
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^D
              – the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
              – enters
              %em only
              in determining the nature of this isomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ι
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota^\ast
              (the time evolution away from the Cauchy surface), but does not affect the nature of the initial value data (of the canonical phase space) as such.
          %p
            It is this “decoupling”
            %em on the canonical phase space
            of the gravity/metric effects from the phase space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gau%C3%9F+law"} Gauß law
            constraint which allows to gain plenty of insight into brane configurations from purely cohomological analysis of fluxes on Cauchy surfaces, disregarding the full solution of the coupled (super-)gravity equations of motion:
          %h3#BraneIntersectionsImprintedOnFlux Qualitative solutions: Brane intersections
          %p
            Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SolutionSpaceViaGaussLaw"}
            implies that on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globally+hyperbolic+spacetimes"} globally hyperbolic spacetimes
            the structure of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            flux densities in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            may be analyzed already by solving the (non-linear)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+law"} Gauss law
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GaussLawFromBianchiIdentities"} (14)
            for duality-symmetric fluxes on any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
            and ignoring the coupling to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            there (assuming only that there exists at least one gravitational field configuration which solves its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
            with source terms of this form). Since the same Gauss law also governs the admissible flux quantization laws
            %a{:href => "#FluxQuantizationLaws"} below
            we showcase a couple of qualitative solutions to highlight just how much non-trivial (brane-)physics is encoded in these equations.
          %p
          #D6CreationAndHananyWittenEffect.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-D8+brane+bound+state"} D6/D8-intersections
                and the
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hanany-Witten+effect"} Hanany-Witten effect
              A popular conjecture by
              %a{:href => "#Hanany-Witten+effect#HananyWitten97"} Hanany &amp; Witten 1997
              states that the expected
              = succeed "-branes" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} D
                        %mrow
                          %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                          %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{D}_{\!p}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} NS
                      %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{NS}_5
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} D
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{D}_{p+2}
              (cf.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#HigherFluxAndItsBranes"} above
              = succeed "-branes" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} D
                        %mrow
                          %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                          %mi p
                          %mo +
                          %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{D}_{\!p+2}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} NS
                        %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{NS}_5
              %a{:href => "Hanany-Witten+effect#Marolf01"} Marolf 2001 (§2)
              that this
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hanany-Witten+effect"} Hanany-Witten effect
              should be understandable entirely from analysis of the flux Bianchi identities
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GaussLawFromBianchiIdentities"} (14)
            %p
              For the case of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-D8+brane+bound+states"} NS5/D6/D8-brane intersections
              \&amp;lbrack;e.g.
              = succeed ";" do
                %a{:href => "Hanany-Witten+effect#HananyZaffaroni98"} Hanany &amp; Zaffaroni 1998 (§2.4)
              %a{:href => "Hanany-Witten+effect#BergshoeffLozanoOrtin98"} Bergshoeff, Lozano &amp; Ortin 1998 (p. 60)
              this may be seen as follows (the other cases work analogously):
            %p
              Here, by the flux equation
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:TwistedUnboundedRREquations"} (9)
            #eq:EquationsForHananyWittenEffectForD6D8.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (15)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mn 0
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo =
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mn 0
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo ,
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mn 2
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo =
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mn 0
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{array}{l}
                    \\mathrm{d} F_0 \;=\; 0
                    \\,,
                    \\\
                    \\mathrm{d}
                    \\,
                    F_2
                    \\;=\;
                    H_3 \wedge F_0
                    \\,,
                    \\end{array}
            %p
              the flux density
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0
              of
              = succeed "-branes" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} D
                        %mn 8
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{D}_8
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+constant+function"} locally constant function
              that vanishes in the vacuum and jumps by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              units across the singular locus of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D8-branes"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} D
                        %mn 8
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{D}_8
                \-branes
              (cf. e.g.
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "NS5-brane#Fazzi17"} Fazzi 2017 (p. 40)
            .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -20px -40px 20px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/FluxesOfD6BetweenNS5AndD8-230618b.jpg", :width => "580px"}/
            %p
              (1.) When the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NS5-brane"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} NS
                        %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{NS}_5
                \-brane
              is located in the vacuum where
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0 = 0
              = succeed "-flux" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{2}
              = succeed "-factor" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:EquationsForHananyWittenEffectForD6D8"} (15)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
              vanishes at infinity then the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PDE"} PDE
              demands it vanishes everywhere, reflecting the absence of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-branes"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} D
                          %mn 6
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{D}_6
                  \-branes
            %p
              (2.) When the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NS5-brane"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} NS
                        %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{NS}_5
                \-brane
              is located on the other side of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D8-brane"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} D
                          %mn 8
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{D}_8
                  \-branes
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_0 = N
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:EquationsForHananyWittenEffectForD6D8"} (15)
              shows that
              = succeed "-flux/" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_2
              = succeed "-number" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-brane"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} D
                          %mn 6
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{D}_6
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mn 9
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^9
              orthogonal to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D8-branes"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} D
                        %mn 8
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{D}_8
                \-branes
              in proportionality to the
              = succeed "-component" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mn 9
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d}x^9
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_3
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NS5-brane"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} NS
                        %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{NS}_5
                \-brane
              locus.
            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2%2FM5-brane+bound+state"}
                  M2
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊥
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \perp
                  M5-brane intersections
                on “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-strings"}> M-strings
