              to around either of the punctures (the brane loci) in the 2-punctured plane. Here:
            %ul
              %li
                %p the strength of the circular lines indicates the absolute value of the flux density sourced by the respective 5-brane,
              %li
                %p the arrows indicate the orientation of the flux density of either 5-brane,
              %li
                %p the parallelograms indicate the orientation of their wedge product.
            %p Evidently, the absolute value of the wedge product is concentrated near the 5-branes and particularly between them…
            .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -35px -10px 0px 10px"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-SourcingM2FluxFromM5Fluxes.jpg", :width => "670px"}/
            %p
              …but the orientation of the wedge product changes sign across the axis connecting the branes, as shown. This means that the flux
              %em sourced
              by this wedge product, according to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:M2FluxSourcedSourceByTwoM5Branes"} (16)
            %p
              This sourced flux concentration (indicated in red) witnesses an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
              stretching between the two
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-branes"} M5-branes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+intersection"} intersection
              is known as the
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-string"} M-string
            %p
          %p
            Notice how in these examples we
            %em chose
            integral values for the total source brane fluxes. Next we discuss how such
            %em flux quantization
            is systematically enforced in the higher gauge theory.
          %h2#FluxQuantizationLaws Flux/Charge quantization laws
          %p
            With the solution space (Prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SolutionSpaceViaGaussLaw"}
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HigherMaxwellEquations"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+densities"} flux densities
            such that the total fluxes and their total source charges take values in some discrete space.
          %p The technical issue to be resolved here is that:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                this is a
                %em global condition
                on the flux densities: The local flux densities may take any value (compatible with the equations of motion) and yet the total accumulation of all these local contributions needs to be constrained;
            %li
              %p
                the evident idea of constraining the ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integrals
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+densities"} flux densities
                (their “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periods"}> periods
                ”) makes sense only for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+differential+forms"} closed differential forms
                and hence does not work for non-linear Bianchi identities (such as those of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} C-field
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MotionOfSupergravityCField"}
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MotionOfCoupledBRRFields"}
          %p To resolve this, one may first observe that:
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: 0px -10px 0px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-IntegralFlux2.jpg", :width => "380px"}/
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration+of+differential+forms"} integrals
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/periods"} periods
                of ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+differential+form"} closed
                differential
                = succeed "-forms" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n
                over
                = succeed "-manifolds" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+bijection"} natural correspondence
                with their
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+class"} classes
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo ∈
                        %msubsup
                          %mi H
                          %mi dR
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [F_n] \in H^n_{dR}(-)
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concordance"} concordance
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} classes
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi H
                          %mi dR
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo ≃
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ω
                          %mi dR
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi clsd
                        %msub
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} /
                          %mi cncrdnc
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^n_{dR}(-) \,\simeq\, \Omega^n_{dR}(-)_{clsd}\big/_{cncrdnc}
            %li
              %p
                so that integrality of closed flux density
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_n
                is witnessed by an integral cohomology class
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi χ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ;
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\chi] \in H^n(X;\mathbb{Z})
                whose “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+theorem"}> de Rham character
                ” image
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ch
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi χ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ∈
                      %msubsup
                        %mi H
                        %mi dR
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ch[\chi] \in H^n_{dR}(X)
                coincides with the deformation class
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi F
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [F_n]
          %p
            and then that this perspective generalizes &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#FSS23"}> FSS23-Char
            ,
            = succeed "&rbrack;:" do
              %a{:href => "#SS23-FQ"} SS23-FQ
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -35px -10px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-StepsInFluxQuantization.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %p
            (1) Higher Maxwell-type equations
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:EqHigherMaxwellEquations"} (6)
            have a
            %strong
              characteristic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
                \-algebra
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra+valued+differential+forms"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝔞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
                \-valued differential forms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+law"} Gauss law
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GaussLawFromBianchiIdentities"} (14)
            is equivalently the condition that these be
            %em closed
            (i.e.:
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+L-infinity-algebra+valued+differential+form"} flat
            = succeed "\u201D;" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maurer-Cartan+element"} Maurer-Cartan element
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity"} Italian SuGra literature
            %br/
            (2.) Also (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:HomeoToHomotopyType"} (37)
            of)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            (under mild conditions) have a characteristic
            = succeed "-algebra:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            = succeed "-rational" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+L-infinity+algebra"}
                  Whitehead bracket
                  = succeed "-algebra" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi L
                            %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %br/
            (3.) The
            %strong nonabelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Dold+character+map"} Chern-Dold character map
            turns
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+L-infinity+algebra+valued+differential+forms"}
                closed
                = succeed "-valued" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔩
                        %mi 𝒜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{l}\mathcal{A}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} =
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} KU
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{KU}_0
            %br/
            (4.) The
            %strong possible flux quantization laws
            for a higher gauge field are those spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            whose Whitehead
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %br/
            (5.) Given a flux quantization law
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potentials"} gauge potentials
            are deformations of the flux densities into characters of a
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            = succeed "-cohomology." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            %br/
            (It is not obvious that this reduces to the usual notion of gauge potentials, but it does.)
            %br/
            (6.) These non-perturbatively completed higher gauge fields form a
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"} smooth higher groupoid
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+differential+cohomology"}
                differential
                = succeed "-cohomology" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒜
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of the flux-quantized higher gauge theory.
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-CanonNonabDiffCohomAsPhaseSpace2.jpg", :width => "870px"}/
          %p
            This flux-quantized phase space hence subsumes the “solitonic” fields with non-trivial charge sectors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi = 0
          %p
            Incidentally, it follows (as discussed below) that the choice of flux quantization law
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            not only defines the solitonic content of the theory but completely characterizes it:
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -35px -10px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-TopologicalPhaseSpace.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"} shape
            (topological realization) of this phase space stack is the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong space of topological fields
          %p
            which implies that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary homology
            of the phase space stack constitutes the
            %strong topological quantum observables
            on the higher gauge theory.
          %p
            Hence if one focuses only on the solitonic or
            = succeed "-content" do
              %em topological field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
              \-cohomology
            moduli of the Cauchy surface, with the full phase space stack serving to justify this object.
          %p
            %br/
          %p We now explain all this in more detail:
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#TotalFluxInNonabelianDeRhamCohomology Total flux as Non-abelian de Rham cohomology
          %p
            We explain how higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bianchi+identities"} Bianchi identities
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:EqHigherMaxwellEquations"} (6)
            and their corresponding higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+laws"} Gauss laws
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GaussLawFromBianchiIdentities"} (14)
            are equivalently the closure (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+L-infinity+algebra+valued+differential+form"}> flatness
            ) condition on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra+valued+differential+form"} differential forms valued in
            a characteristic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              \-algebra
            (Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SolutionsAsFlatForms"}
            below), so that
            %em total flux
            is a class in
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+de+Rham+cohomology"} nonabelian de Rham cohomology
            (Def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NonabelianDeRhamCohomology"}
            below).
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            The notion of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %em strong homotopy Lie algebra
            is finally becoming more widely appreciated in physics, where they appear in various guises (see the references
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "L-infinity-algebra#ReferencesInPhysics"} there
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+L-infinity+algebra"} nilpotent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connective+chain+complex"} connective
            (iii) of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+type"} finite type
            %em joint
            incarnation as higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+density"} flux density
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            %em and
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+L-infinity+algebra"} higher Whitehead brackets
            (all to be explained in a moment), which one might refer to as the
          %p
            %strong Flux Homotopy Lie algebra triality.
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px -10px 10px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-FluxHomotopyLieTriality.jpg", :width => "380px"}/
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            With
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy
            we are referring here specifically the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+theorem+of+dg-algebraic+rational+homotopy+theory"} fundamental theorem of dg-algebraic rational homotopy theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FSS23"} FSS23-Char, §5
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            by the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%27Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity"} FDA method in supregravity
            we are referring, with some hindsight, to the observations of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "D'Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity#Nieuwenhuizen82"} van Nieuwenhuizen 1983
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "D'Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity#DAuriaFre82"} D Auria &amp; Fré 1982
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "D'Auria-Fre+formulation+of+supergravity#CastellaniDAuriaFre"} Castellani, D’Auria &amp; Fré
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+fundamental+super+p-branes#FSS13"} FSS13
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+fundamental+super+p-branes#FSS16b"} FSS18
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "geometry+of+physics+--+fundamental+super+p-branes#HSS19"} HSS19
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FSS19HigherStruc"}
                FSS19-
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℚ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
                Struc
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Dold+character"} nonabelian character
            is the generalization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Dold+character"} Chern-Dold character
            map from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology"} Whitehead-generalized cohomology
            to generalized
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FSS23"} FSS23-Char
          %p
            In particular, this means that
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %em not
            directly to be understood as generalizations of the gauge Lie algebras familiar from Yang-Mills theory, which are coefficients of the gauge potentials, but instead as the coefficients of their flux densities (cf. Rem.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RoleOfLInfinityAlgebras"}
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              \-Algebras.
            Since we are assuming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebras"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              \-algebras
            to be connective and of finite type (meaning that they are degreewise finite-dimensional and concentrated in non-negative degrees) we may
            %em define
            them through their Chevalley-Eilenberg (CE) algebras in the following manner, which is not only convenient for dealing with the otherwise intricate sign rules, but also essential to their alternative perspectives in the above triality:
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;
            %strong Chevalley-Eilenberg algebras of Lie algebras.
            Namely, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            a finite-dimensional Lie algebra (our ground field is the real numbers, throughout) with Lie bracket a skew-symmetric linear map
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo →
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-] \,:\, \mathfrak{g} \otimes \mathfrak{g} \to \mathfrak{g}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝔤
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}^\ast
            is equipped with the dual bracket
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
