                \-algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+L-infinity+algebra"}
              Whitehead
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4-sphere"} 4-sphere
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+supergravity"} D=11 supergravity
            %em gauge algebra of the C-field
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#CremmerJuliaLuPope98"}> Cremmer, Julia, Lu &amp; Pope 1998, (2.6)
            ;
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Sati10"} Sati 2010, §4
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#SatiVoronov22"} Sati &amp; Voronov 2022, (13)
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "D=11+N=1+supergravity#ReferencesCFieldGaugeAlgebra"} there
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-CFieldGaugeAlgebraAsWhiteheadBracket.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (prefactors in Sullivan algebras)
              %br/
              As stated so far, the ubiquituous prefactor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\tfrac{1}{2}
              is pure convention, due to the freedom of rescaling generators by rational (or even real) numbers while retaining dga-isomorphy. However, this factor is fixed by requiring certain integrality properties of the generators, see
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#FSS21WZ"} FSS21-HopfWZ, Prop. 4.6
            %p
              This becomes relevant when regarding the lift back from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔩
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{l}S^4
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                    %mo ≡
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A} \equiv S^4
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+quantization"} flux quantization
              law, because then it implies that the C-field flux densities
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_4
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_7
              in the image of the normalized generators
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_4
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mn 7
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega_7
              satisfy expected integrality conditions &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#FSS21WZ"}> FSS21-HopfWZ, Thm. 4.8
              \&amp;rbrack;. We discuss this further
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "#CFieldFluxQuantizationIn10d"} below
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Rational homotopy theory: Discrading torsion in nonabelian cohomology.
            From the perspective (
            %a{:href => "#ClassifyingSpacesForGeneralizedCohomology"}> above
            ) that any topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            serves as the
            %em classifying space
            of a generalized
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology+theory"} nonabelian cohomology theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+homotopy+theory"} rational homotopy theory
            (survey in
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "rational+homotopy+theory#Hess06"} Hess 2006
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#FSS23"} FSS23-Char, §4
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+subgroup"} torsion
            content of such a cohomology theory, which we will see is, over
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+de+Rham+cohomology"} non-abelian de Rham cohomology
            (Def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NonabelianDeRhamCohomology"}
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔩
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{l}\mathcal{A}
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-HomotopyToCohomology.jpg", :width => "660px"}/
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-RationalizingHomotopyGroups-240204.jpg", :width => "900px"}/
          %p
            Hence to have a classifying space for the non-torsion part of
            = succeed "-cohomology" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
          #eq:RationalizationOfASpace.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (23)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-RationalizationOfASpace.jpg", :width => "650px"}/
          %p
            For example, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rationalization"} rationalization
            of an integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+space"} Eilenberg-MacLane space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi n
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ≡
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^n \mathbb{Z} \,\equiv\, K(\mathbb{Z},n)
            classifies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+cohomology"} rational cohomology
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham cohomology
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-CharacterInOrdinaryCohomology.jpg", :width => "900px"}/
          %p
            We may regard this as the archetype of a
            %em character map
            and ask for its generalization to any
            = succeed "-cohomology" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            %a{:href => "#FSS23"} FSS23-Char
            is that for this purpose one may invoke the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+theorem+of+dg-algebraic+rational+homotopy+theory"}> fundamental theorem of dg-algebraic rational homotopy theory
              \:
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong The Fundamental Theorem of dg-Algebraic Rational Homotopy Theory
            (review in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#FSS23"} FSS23-Char, Prop. 5.6
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+spaces"} rational spaces
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simply-connected+topological+space"} simply-connected
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"} fin-dim
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+cohomology"} rational cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+L-infinity+algebra"}
              Whitehead
              = succeed "-algebras" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:WhiteheadLInfinityAlgebra"} (22)
            over the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
          %p
            In particular, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
