          %p
            Observing that the right-hand side looks close to the definition of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+de+Rham+cohomology"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔩
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{l}\mathcal{A}
              \-valued de Rham cohomology
            (Def.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#NonabelianDeRhamCohomology"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            scalars from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            to the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-RealRationalizationOfASpace.jpg", :width => "530px"}/
          %p#RationalizationOverTheReals
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;
            %strong Rational homotopy theory over the Reals.
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "rational+homotopy+theory#BousfieldGugenheim76"}> Bousfield &amp; Gugenheim 1976
            ; reviewed in
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#FSS23"} FSS23-Char, Def. 5.7, Rem. 5.2, Prop. 5.8
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RationalizationOfASpace"} (23)
            also works over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            (but is then not a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization+of+spaces"}> localization
            ”) to give the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
                \-rationalization
          %p
            With this “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension+of+scalars"} extension of scalars
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#FSS23"} FSS23-Char, Lem 5.3
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a smooth manifold, the fundamental theorem
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:FundamentalTheoremOfRHT"} (24)
            does relate to smooth differential forms &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#FSS23"}> FSS23-Char, Lem. 6.4
            \&amp;rbrack; via a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+de+Rham+theorem"} non-abelian de Rham theorem
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#FSS23"}> FSS23-Char, Thm. 6.5
            \&amp;rbrack;:
          #eq:NonabelianDeRhamTheorem.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (25)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -15px 0px 5px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-NonabelianDeRhamTheorem.jpg", :width => "860px"}/
          %p
            %strong In abelian (ie. Whitehead-generalized) cohomology
            theories both the rationalization step and the subsequent extension of scalars to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            can be more easily described as forming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficient"} coefficient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            with the rational
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"} Eilenberg-MacLane spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H\mathbb{R}
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#FSS23"}> FSS23-Char, Ex. 5.7
            \&amp;rbrack;. This is how the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Dold+character"} Chern-Dold character
            map over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is tacitly used in all the literature on abelian (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology"}> Whitehead-generalized
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            theory (e.g.
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "twisted+differential+cohomology#BunkeNikolaus14"} Bunke &amp; Nikolaus 2014, Def. 4.2
          #eq:RealificationOfSpectra.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (26)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-RealRationalizationOfSpectra.jpg", :width => "530px"}/
          %p
            The point of the non-abelian de Rham theorem
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NonabelianDeRhamTheorem"} (25)
            is to generalize the realifification
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RealificationOfSpectra"} (26)
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead-generalized+cohomology"} Whitehead-generalized cohomology
            to generalized
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+cohomology"} non-abelian cohomology
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy"} Cohomotopy
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:FundamentalTheoremOfRHT"} (24)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra+valued+differential+forms"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              \-valued differential forms
            relate fluxes to their flux-quantization laws.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong The general non-abelian character map
            is now immediate &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#FSS23"}> FSS23-Char, Def. IV.2
            \&amp;rbrack;: It is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+operation"} cohomology operation
            induced by
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rationalization"} rationalization
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-abelian+de+Rham+theorem"} non-abelian de Rham theorem
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NonabelianDeRhamTheorem"} (25)
          #eq:NonabelianCharacterMap.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (27)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-NonabelianCharacterMap.jpg", :width => "900px"}/
          %p
            All the classical abelian character maps
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ExamplesOfCharacterMaps"} (21)
            are special cases of this generalized nonabelian character &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#FSS23"}> FSS23-Char, §7
            \&amp;rbrack;, but now examples in generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            are also included; for instance there is a character map on
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy"} Cohomotopy
            = succeed "&rbrack;" do
              %a{:href => "#FSS23"} FSS23-Char, Ex. 6.11
          %p
            %strong Flux quantization in generalized nonabelian cohomology.
            With the generalized nonabelian character map
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NonabelianCharacterMap"} (27)
            in hand, we may finally state the general concept of flux quantization (to be further refined
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#PhaseSpacesAsDifferentialNonabelianCohomology"} below
          %p
            Recalling
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ConcordanceOfFlatDifferentialForms"} (20)
            that the
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+de+Rham+cohomology"} nonabelian de Rham cohomology
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
            encodes the
            %em total flux
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
            characterized by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebra"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              \-algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{a}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔩
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝔞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{l}\mathcal{A} \,\simeq\, \mathfrak{a}
            the nonabelian character map
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:NonabelianCharacterMap"} (27)
            may be understood as assigning to
            %em discrete charges
            embodied by
            = succeed "-cohomology-classes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
          #eq:NonabelianCharacterMapChargesToFluxes.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (28)
            %code.maruku-mathml
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-CharacterMapChargesToFluxes-240202.jpg", :width => "450px"}/
          %p
            Since the total charges in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi d
                  %mo ;
                  %mi Ω
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^1\big(X^d; \Omega \mathcal{A})\big)
            on the left form a discrete set, we may think of implementing global flux quantization in
            = succeed "-cohomology" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting"} lifting
            of total fluxes through the character map:
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-GlobalFluxQuantization.jpg", :width => "900px"}/
          %p
            Notice that such a lift is not
            %em just
            a (quantization/discretization-)
            %em condition
            on the total fluxes, but also
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            = succeed "-component" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+subgroup"} torsion
            = succeed "-cohomology:" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
          %div{:style => "margin: -30px 0px 20px 0px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/GeomPhys-CharacterKernelAndCokernel.jpg", :width => "900px"}/
          %p
            However, in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
            it is unnatural to have
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extra+structure"} extra structure
            given by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            of gauge equivalence classes, instead one should consider an explicit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
            between actual fields. Doing so leads to emergence of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potentials"} gauge potentials
            and of the higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            stack of the theory, in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#PhaseSpacesAsDifferentialNonabelianCohomology"} next subsection
          %p
          #FluxQuantizationLawsForOrdinaryElectromagnetism.num_remark
            %h6 Example
            %p
              %strong (flux quantization laws for ordinary electromagnetism)
              %br/
            %p
              By Ex.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SolutionSpaceOfElectromagnetism"}
              the characteristic
              = succeed "-algebra" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+Lie+2-algebra"} line Lie 2-algebra
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mi 𝔲
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \mathfrak{u}(1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+L-infinity+algebra"}
                Whitehead
                = succeed "-algebra" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi L
                          %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BU%28n%29"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B \mathrm{U}(1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ≃
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mn 2
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,\simeq\, B^2 \mathbb{Z}
              and hence of its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rationalization"} rationalization
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mn 2
                      %mi ℚ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^2 \mathbb{Q}
            %p Therefore – among many further variants – there are the following choices of flux quantization laws for electromagnetism:
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th EM flux quantization law
                  %th comment
              %tbody
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi B
                                  %mn 2
                                %mi ℚ
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mi mag
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo ×
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi B
                                  %mn 2
                                %mi ℚ
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mi el
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{mag}{\underbrace{B^2 \mathbb{Q}}} \,\times\, \underset{el}{\underbrace{B^2 \mathbb{Q}}}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    this choice imposes
                    %em no
                    flux quantization (it does rule out
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irrational+number"} irrational
                    total fluxes) and as such was the tacit choice since
                    %a{:href => "Maxwell's+equations#Maxwell1865"} Maxwell 1865
                    until
                    %a{:href => "#Dirac31"} Dirac 1931
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi B
                                  %mn 2
                                %mi ℤ
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mi mag
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo ×
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi B
                                  %mn 2
                                %mi ℚ
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mi el
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{mag}{\underbrace{B^2 \mathbb{Z}}} \,\times\, \underset{el}{\underbrace{B^2 \mathbb{Q}}}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    this choice imposes integrality of magentic charge but no further condition on electric flux – common choice since
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a{:href => "#Dirac31"} Dirac 1931
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %a{:href => "#Alvarez85b"} Alvarez 1985b, p. 299
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %a{:href => "Dirac+charge+quantization#Brylinski93"} Brylinski 1993, §7.1
                    %a{:href => "#Freed00"} Freed 2000, Ex. 2.1.2
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi B
                                  %mn 2
                                %mi ℤ
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mi mag
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo ×
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi B
                                  %mn 2
                                %mi ℤ
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mi el
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{mag}{\underbrace{B^2 \mathbb{Z}}} \,\times\, \underset{el}{\underbrace{B^2 \mathbb{Z}}}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    this choice imposes integrality of both magentic and electric charges – considered in
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a{:href => "#FreedMooreSegal07a"} Freed, Moore &amp; Segal 2007a
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %a{:href => "#FreedMooreSegal07b"} 2007b
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %a{:href => "#BeckerBeniniSchenkelSzabo15"} Becker, Benini, Schenkel &amp; Szabo 2015, Rem. 2.3
                    = succeed ";" do
                      %a{:href => "#LazaroiuShahbazi22"} Lazaroiu &amp; Shahbazi 2022
                    %a{:href => "#LazaroiuShahbazi23"} Lazaroiu &amp; Shahbazi 2023, §2
                %tr
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %msup
                                  %mi B
                                  %mn 2
                                %mi ℤ
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mi mag
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo ⋊
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %mi B
                                %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                                %mi K
                                %mo ⋉
                                %mi B
                                %mi ℤ
                                %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mi el
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{mag}{\underbrace{B^2 \mathbb{Z}}} \,\rtimes\, \underset{el}{\underbrace{B\big( K \ltimes B\mathbb{Z} \big) }}
                  %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                    for a
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi K
                          %mo →
                          %mi Aut
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ℤ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \to Aut(\mathbb{Z})
                    – this choice induces non-commutativity between EL/EL- and EL/M-fluxes, an example of a “non-evident” flux quantization condition considered in
                    %a{:href => "#SS23-FQ"} SS23-FQ
            %p
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#PhaseSpacesAsDifferentialNonabelianCohomology Phase spaces as Differential nonabelian cohomology
          %p
            With higher Maxwell-type equations of flux given (
            %a{:href => "#EquationsOfMotionOfFlux"}> above
            ) and with a compatible flux/charge quantization law
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            chosen (
            %a{:href => "#TotalFluxInNonabelianDeRhamCohomology"}> above
            ), we explain here how the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            content of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
            (including the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potentials"} gauge potentials
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CanonicalAndCovariantPhaseSpace"} (13)
            appears as the corresponding “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"}> moduli space
            ” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+differential+cohomology"} nonabelian differential cohomology
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi A
                    %mo ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{A}
            of any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
          %p
            In fact, such a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            is not just a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-groupoid"}
                smooth
                = succeed "-groupoid" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn ∞
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            (aka
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+infinity-stack"}
                  smooth
                  = succeed "-stack" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+morphisms"} higher morphisms
            represent the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+transformations"} higher gauge transformations
            between the field configurations; and so we briefly review some required concepts from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+theory"} higher topos theory
            (for full details we refer the reader to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FSS23Char"} FSS23-Char, §1 &amp; §9
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Schreiber24"} Schreiber 2024
          %p
            However, the “topological observables” on the higher gauge theory (those that detect topological charge structure but not the local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            of field configurations) depend only on the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shape+modality"}> shape
            ” of the phase space stack, which by the properties of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28infinity%2C1%29-topos"}> cohesive higher topos theory
            ” turns out to coincide simply with the actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
            from the Cauchy surface into the classifying space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
          %p
            Therefore the reader who is not to be bothered with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28infinity%2C1%29-topos+theory"} higher topos theory
            and is content with the “topological” implications of flux quantization may safely (dis-)regard this subsection as a black box which guarantees that once a classifying spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            is chosen for flux quantization, it controls not only the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi d
                  %mo ;
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ≡
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 0
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H^1\big(X^d;\, \mathcal{A}\big) \,\equiv\, \pi_0 Map(X^d, \mathcal{A})
            of total charges of the theory, but also the full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(X^d, \mathcal{A})
            of local charges.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %strong Smooth sets of moduli of flux densities.
          %p
            The idea of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stacks"} moduli stacks
            is the evident refinement of that of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+spaces"} classifying spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} structure
            (in physics typically: a certain kind of fields):
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                a
                %em classifying space
                is such that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+classes"} homotopy classes
                of (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"}> continuous
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maps"} maps
                to it from any
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                correspond to
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+classes"} equivalence classes
                of such structures on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                a
                %em moduli stack
                is such that the individual (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+map"}> smooth
                ) maps to it from any
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                correspond to the individual such structures on
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %p
                with (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopy"}> higher
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
                of these maps corresponding to the (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+transformation"}> higher
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
                between these structures.
          %p
            In order to understand that and how such moduli stacks may exist at all, it is useful to take a perspective where
            %em every
            kind of space under consideration (such as spacetime itself) is
            %em defined
            (not as an underlying set of points with extra structure but) by the maps it receives from given
            = succeed "." do
              %em probes
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
            %strong Smooth sets.
            We refer the reader to the section
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets"} geometry of physics – smooth sets
            or else to &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#GiotopoulosSati23"}> Giotopoulos &amp; Sati 2023
            \&amp;rbrack; for more exposition and discssion of the following idea:
          %p
            For the present purpose of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+differential+geometry"} higher differential geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            which qualifies as a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+set"}> smooth set
            ” should be
            %em defined
            (not necessarily by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of points equipped with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            but) by a system of sets of ways
          #eq:PlotsByCartesianSpaces.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (29)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
