                %a{:href => "#RRFieldFluxQuantization"} below
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#background_fluxes_as_twisting_of_cohomology Background fluxes as Twisting of cohomology
          %p (…)
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#Examples Examples in String/M-Theory
          %p
            While flux quantization is an issue in any higher gauge theory, the examples where it has received most (essentially all) of the attention are those of evident relevance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            — which is what we focus on in the following.
          %p
            While
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Polyakov+gauge-string+duality"} an attempt to understand
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap+problem"} all-important but elusive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative behaviour
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theories"} Yang-Mills theories
            (notably
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} color
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
            as endpoints of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+strings"} open strings
            stuck on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+brane+model"} intersecting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
            in an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Randall-Sundrum+model"} unobserved higher dimensional spacetime
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} their scattering amplitudes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            in ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline+formalism"} worldline
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            of a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+SCFT"} 2d SCFT
          %p
            But since flux quantization laws (as discussed
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#FluxQuantizationLaws"} above
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+fields"} background fields
            = succeed "/" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
          %p
            For instance, the traditional
            %em Hypothesis K
            (
            %a{:href => "#RRFieldFluxQuantization"}> below
            ) that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            fluxes are quantized in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            has been motivated/justified &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Witten98"}> Witten 1998, §3)
            \&amp;rbrack; as describing – or in fact pre-scribing – the stable end results of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tachyon+condensation"} tachyon condensation
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+string"} open string
            modes stretching between
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+D-brane"} anti D-brane
            pairs, a process which cannot be followed by string perturbation theory, but which is expected (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sen%27s+conjecture"}> Sen's conjecture
            ” &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "Sen's+conjecture#Sen98"}> Sen 1998
            \&amp;rbrack;) to find the non-perturbative true
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
            where
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane"} D-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+D-brane"} anti D-brane
            pairs have mutually annihilated as far as possible. Indeed, at least in practice, RR-field flux quantization in topological K-theory has become the widely-accepted
            %em definition
            of stable D-brane vacua, and as such must be understood as a partial proposal for the nature of non-perturbative string theory.
          %p
            On the other hand, strongly-coupled string theory at large-scale/low-energy is also
            %a{:href => "M-theory#Hints"} famously argued
            to be described by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+supergravity"} D=11 supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            in 11d should go further still towards the full non-perturbative definition of string theory (hence of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            %a{:href => "#FluxQuantizationLaws"} above
            provides a systematic mathematical theory which clearly delineates the available choices of non-perturbative completions and allows to rigorously derive their consequences.
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#RRFieldFluxQuantization B-&amp;RR-Field flux quantization in 10d
          %p
            Recall the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauss+law"} Gauss law
            of the unbounded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            flux densities (Ex.
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#MotionOfUnboundedRRFields"}
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SolutionSpaceViaGaussLaw"}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/massive+type+IIA+supergravity"} massive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+supergravity"} type IIA supergravity
            and
            %em ignoring
            (as commonly done, but see below… for possible justification) the non-linear Bianchi identity
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:BRRFieldEquationsOfMotion"} (12)
            of the dual B-field flux
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 7
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_7
            (whence we now notationally suppress
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 7
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_7
            altogether, as usual):
          #eq:SolutionSpaceForUnboundedRRRecalled.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (43)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SolSpace
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi H
                            %mn 3
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo ∈
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %msubsup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mi dR
                            %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mn 9
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mo •
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo ∈
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %msubsup
                            %mi Ω
                            %mi dR
                            %mrow
                              %mn 2
                              %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mn 9
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtext s.t.
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %msub
